# PLACEHOLDER — NOT A REAL KEY. The backup job refuses to run while this line is here.
#
# REPLACE: generate the keypair with `age-keygen -o backup.age.key`, commit ONLY the
# public half below, and keep the private half offline with the CTO and escrowed with
# the faculty adviser (docs/runbooks/02-RESTORE_FROM_BACKUP.md,
# docs/runbooks/03-CREDENTIAL_ROTATION.md).
#
# WHY THIS FILE SHIPS EMPTY. A keypair generated by the build would mean the private
# half existed, however briefly, somewhere other than the CTO's own custody — and a
# backup encrypted to a key somebody else generated is not a backup the org controls.
# The security property here is custody, not ciphertext, so the person who will hold
# the key has to be the person who makes it. An empty placeholder that fails loudly is
# honest; a working key with unclear provenance is not.
#
# WHAT THIS KEY PROTECTS. The nightly dump is every scholar's birthdate, address,
# contact number and school ID number. An UNENCRYPTED dump of 600 scholars' PII sitting
# in object storage IS ITSELF THE BREACH the backup exists to survive
# (ARCHITECTURE.md §1, RA 10173 / CBL Art. VIII §6).
#
# THE PUBLIC HALF IS SAFE TO COMMIT. It can only encrypt. Anyone who has it can create a
# backup nobody but the CTO can read, which is the whole design.
#
# Replace everything below this line with the single `age1...` line from age-keygen.
age1REPLACE_ME_WITH_THE_REAL_PUBLIC_KEY_SEE_COMMENT_ABOVE
